  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Find the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}(x)$ of $f(x)={e}^{3x}$ about $x=0.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{\u2032}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{\u2033}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(0),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{3x} & \u27f9 &  & f(0) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2032}(0) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =9{e}^{3x} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2033}(0) & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(0),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+3x+\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
